## Switch expressions (Java 14+)

```java
String category = switch (status) {
    case 200, 201, 204 -> "success";
    case 404           -> "not found";
    default            -> "other";
};
```

Several labels can share one arm, as in `case 200, 201, 204 ->`. With this switch a status of 404 gives `"not found"`, and only a value that matches no label reaches `default`. A switch over a `String` compares the selector with `equals`, so text read from a request matches its literal label.

- The **arrow form** never falls through: exactly one arm runs.
- A switch **expression** must be exhaustive: over an `enum` you can list every constant and omit `default`; adding a constant later makes the switch a compile error until you handle it.
- An arm that needs several statements uses a block and `yield`:

```java
int fee = switch (tier) {
    case STANDARD -> 499;
    case EXPRESS -> {
        int base = 999;
        yield orderCents >= 10_000 ? base / 2 : base;
    }
    case PICKUP -> 0;
};
```

## The old colon form falls through

```java
switch (n) {
    case 1: out.append("one ");
    case 2: out.append("two "); break;   // n == 1 prints "one two": no break after case 1
    default: out.append("other");
}
```

Prefer the arrow form in new code. Switching on a **null** String or enum throws `NullPointerException` (in Java 17), in the colon form and the arrow form alike, so check for null first.

Fall-through is why forgotten-`break` bugs exist: nothing warns you, and the next case's code simply runs. A case that ends in `return` or `throw` needs no `break`. The arrow form has been standard since Java 14, not a preview feature, and the comma list (`case 1, 2 ->`) states the one legitimate use of fall-through, shared code, directly.

## Pattern matching for instanceof (Java 16+)

```java
if (shape instanceof Circle c) return Math.PI * c.radius() * c.radius();
if (obj instanceof String s && !s.isBlank()) use(s);
```

The pattern tests the type AND declares a typed variable, replacing the old test-then-cast pair. The variable is in scope only where the test is known to be true.

That scope follows the control flow. After `if (!(obj instanceof Integer n)) return;` the rest of the method runs only when the match succeeded, so `n` is usable there: `show(41)` prints `42`. The right side of `&&` can use the variable, but `obj instanceof String s || s.isEmpty()` does not compile, because `s` is not bound when the left side is false. A pattern never matches `null` (`null instanceof String s` is `false`), so no separate null check is needed in front of it.

## Sealed hierarchies (Java 17)

```java
sealed interface PaymentResult permits Approved, Declined, NeedsReview {}
record Approved(String authCode) implements PaymentResult {}
record Declined(String reason) implements PaymentResult {}
record NeedsReview(String ticket) implements PaymentResult {}
```

`sealed` lists every allowed subtype, so the set of cases is closed and known to the compiler (pattern matching in `switch` over sealed types became standard in Java 21; in 17 you use instanceof chains or a method on each record).

Each permitted subtype must itself say `final`, `sealed` or `non-sealed`; leaving all three off is a compile error. A class that is not in the `permits` list and tries to implement the interface does not compile either. Records are implicitly final, which is why they fit sealed hierarchies so well, but sealing on its own says nothing about immutability.

## Loops and early exits

- Enhanced `for (var item : items)` for any `Iterable` or array.
- `break` leaves the loop, `continue` skips to the next iteration; a **label** (`outer:`) lets `break outer;` leave nested loops.
- `continue outer;` jumps to the next iteration of the outer loop instead of leaving it.
- Removing from a list inside a for-each over that same list is a classic crash: removing `"a"` from `[a, b, c]` that way throws `ConcurrentModificationException`. Use `list.removeIf(x -> ...)` or an explicit `Iterator` and its `remove`.
- **Guard clauses** keep handlers flat:

```java
if (order == null) return ResponseEntity.notFound().build();
if (order.isShipped()) return ResponseEntity.status(409).build();
// main path, unindented
```

## Classes and encapsulation

```java
public class Order {
    private final long id;                 // final: assigned once, in the constructor
    private final List<Line> lines = new ArrayList<>();
    private Status status = Status.DRAFT;

    public Order(long id) { this.id = id; }

    public void addLine(String sku, int qty) {
        if (status != Status.DRAFT) throw new IllegalStateException("order is " + status);
        if (qty <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("qty must be positive");
        lines.add(new Line(sku, qty));
    }
    public List<Line> lines() { return List.copyOf(lines); }   // callers cannot modify ours
}
```

Fields are `private`; behaviour methods protect the invariants. Returning the internal mutable list would let callers bypass `addLine`. `final` on a field means the reference cannot change; the object it points to can.

If `lines()` returned the field itself, a caller could write `order.lines().add(...)` on a shipped order and skip every check in `addLine`. `List.copyOf(lines)` hands out a snapshot whose `add` throws `UnsupportedOperationException`. `Collections.unmodifiableList(lines)` is the cheaper option: it also refuses `add`, but it is a read-only view, so the caller sees lines added later.

## static

`static` members belong to the class: constants (`static final`), factory methods, pure helpers. Mutable static state is shared by every thread and request: a cache in a static `HashMap` is a race condition and a memory leak waiting to happen (topic 16).

## Records (Java 16+)

```java
public record Money(long cents, String currency) {
    public Money {                                   // compact canonical constructor: validation
        if (cents < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("negative");
        Objects.requireNonNull(currency, "currency");
    }
    public Money plus(Money other) {
        if (!currency.equals(other.currency)) throw new IllegalArgumentException("currency mismatch");
        return new Money(cents + other.cents, currency);
    }
}
new Money(150, "EUR")              // toString: Money[cents=150, currency=EUR]
new Money(1, "EUR").equals(new Money(1, "EUR"))   // true
```

A record declares an immutable data carrier: private final fields, accessor methods named like the components (`cents()`, not `getCents()`), and `equals`, `hashCode` and `toString` based on all components. The **compact constructor** runs validation before the fields are assigned. Records are ideal for DTOs, value objects, events and map keys; they cannot extend other classes, and they are a poor fit for JPA entities (topic 19).

A record may add methods and static factories (`static Money eur(long cents)`), but not instance fields: `private int extra;` inside a record body is a compile error ("field declaration must be static"). All the state is in the components, which is what makes the generated `equals` trustworthy.

## Enums

```java
public enum Status {
    DRAFT("d"), PAID("p"), SHIPPED("s");
    private final String code;
    Status(String code) { this.code = code; }
    public String code() { return code; }
}
Status.valueOf("PAID")   // PAID
Status.valueOf("paid")   // IllegalArgumentException: No enum constant ...
Status.PAID.ordinal()    // 1  (never persist ordinals: reordering constants corrupts data)
```

Enums are full classes: fields, constructors, methods, even per-constant behaviour. Compare them with `==` (each constant is a singleton). `EnumMap` and `EnumSet` are compact and keep the constants' declaration order. Persist enums by **name** (or an explicit code), never by ordinal.

The ordinal is only the position in the declaration. If rows store `SHIPPED` as 2 and someone inserts `PACKED` before it, every stored 2 silently means `PACKED`: inserting or reordering constants changes the stored meaning without any error. In JPA, `@Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)` stores the name, while the default stores the ordinal. `valueOf` is exact too: `Status.valueOf(" PAID")` throws, so normalise input first.

## Choosing

| need | use |
|---|---|
| entity with identity and lifecycle | class |
| immutable value, DTO, event | record |
| a fixed set of named values with behaviour | enum |

In an order system, `Order` is a class: it has an id, a lifecycle and rules that change its state. `Money(150, "EUR")` and an `OrderPlaced` event are records, and `Status` is an enum. An immutable DTO returned by an API is a record with one component per field: value equality comes free, and Jackson serialises the components. A class with public mutable fields lets any caller change the response, and an enum is the wrong tool for per-response data, because its constants are shared singletons.

## Polymorphism: overriding instance methods

```java
class Animal { String speak() { return "..."; } }
class Dog extends Animal { @Override String speak() { return "woof"; } }

Animal a = new Dog();
a.speak();   // "woof": instance methods are virtual in Java (dispatched on the runtime type)
```

All non-static, non-private, non-final instance methods are virtual. `@Override` makes the compiler check that you really override something (a typo in the name or parameters otherwise creates a new, unrelated method).

The classic case is `equals`. `public boolean equals(Order o)` overloads `Object.equals(Object)` instead of overriding it, so a `HashSet` that calls `equals(Object)` keeps two orders with the same id. With `@Override` on it, the same method is a compile error ("method does not override or implement a method from a supertype").

## What is NOT polymorphic

- **Fields**: `a.name` reads `Animal.name` when `a` is declared as `Animal`, even if `Dog` declares its own `name` field. Fields are resolved by the declared type ("hiding"). Never redeclare fields in subclasses.
- **Static methods**: `Dog.kind()` hides `Animal.kind()`; which one runs depends on the type you call it through, not the object.

## Abstract classes and template methods

```java
abstract class Account {
    protected long balance;
    public final void withdraw(long cents) {         // the template: fixed algorithm
        long total = cents + fee(cents);
        if (balance - total < -overdraftLimit()) throw new IllegalStateException("insufficient funds");
        balance -= total;
    }
    protected abstract long fee(long cents);          // steps subclasses supply
    protected long overdraftLimit() { return 0; }     // a default subclasses may override
}
```

An `abstract` class cannot be instantiated and can hold state and constructors. `final` on a method forbids overriding (protecting the algorithm); `final` on a class forbids subclassing (`String` is final).

`new Account(0)` is a compile error ("Account is abstract; cannot be instantiated"), while `new Savings(0)` runs the base constructor through `super`. `final class Money` stops other classes from extending `Money`; it does not make the fields final, which is a separate choice per field. The template method pattern relies on that split: the base class fixes the order of the steps, and subclasses fill in the steps that differ.

## Interfaces

Interfaces define capabilities: `Comparable`, `Runnable`, `PaymentGateway`. A class implements many interfaces but extends one class. Since Java 8 interfaces can have `default` methods (with a body) and `static` methods; since Java 9, private helper methods. Default methods let you evolve an interface without breaking implementers. If a class inherits the same default method from two interfaces, it must override it to resolve the conflict; without the override the class does not compile ("types A and B are incompatible"). The override may delegate to one side with `A.super.hi()`, or combine both.

```java
public interface PaymentGateway {
    Receipt charge(Money amount, String token);
    default boolean supportsRefunds() { return false; }
}
```

## Constructors and super

A subclass constructor calls `super(...)` first (implicitly `super()` if you omit it, which fails to compile when the parent has no no-arg constructor). `super.method()` calls the parent's implementation from an override.

Do not call an overridable method from a constructor. The parent constructor runs before the subclass's constructor body, so the override sees a half-built object: a field that the subclass assigns in its own constructor is still `null` when the parent constructor calls the override. Constructors should only assign fields and check arguments; work that needs a complete object belongs in a method called afterwards.

## Sealed classes (Java 17)

`public sealed abstract class Shape permits Circle, Square {}` restricts who may extend it; permitted subclasses must be `final`, `sealed` or `non-sealed`.

When every subclass is declared in the same file, the `permits` clause may be left out and the compiler infers it: `sealed interface Shape {}` followed by `record Circle(double r) implements Shape {}` and `record Square(double s) implements Shape {}` compiles. A sealed hierarchy suits a closed set of results, such as approved, declined and needs-review payments, where callers must handle every case.

## Prefer composition

Deep hierarchies couple subclasses to their parents' internals. Prefer small interfaces plus composition (a `PriceCalculator` holding a list of `DiscountRule`s) and use inheritance for stable is-a relationships and framework base classes. Program to interfaces: depend on `PaymentGateway`, not `StripeGateway`, so tests and future providers plug in. A test fake can be swapped in for the Stripe adapter without changing a single caller; interface calls are not slower in any way that matters.

## Controllers

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/orders")
public class OrderController {
    private final OrderService service;
    public OrderController(OrderService service) { this.service = service; }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public OrderDto get(@PathVariable long id) { return service.get(id); }   // 200 + JSON

    @GetMapping
    public Page<OrderDto> list(@RequestParam(defaultValue = "0") int page, @RequestParam(required = false) String status) { ... }

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<OrderDto> create(@Valid @RequestBody CreateOrder body) {
        OrderDto created = service.create(body);
        return ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/api/orders/" + created.id())).body(created);   // 201 + Location
    }

    @DeleteMapping("/{id}")
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NO_CONTENT)
    public void delete(@PathVariable long id) { service.delete(id); }
}
```

Return DTOs (records), never JPA entities: entities leak internal fields, trigger lazy-loading errors during serialisation (topic 20) and invite over-posting when used as request bodies.

Each handler above maps to one status the client sees: `get` answers 200 with the DTO as JSON, `ResponseEntity.created(uri)` answers 201 with a `Location` header, and `@ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NO_CONTENT)` makes `delete` answer 204 with no body. `@RequestParam(defaultValue = "0") int page` binds 0 when the parameter is missing; without a default, a missing required parameter is a 400. A record such as `record OrderDto(long id, String status, long totalCents)` exposes exactly the fields the API promises.

## Status codes Spring produces for you

- No handler for the path: **404**. A handler exists for the path but not the method: **405**.
- `@PathVariable long id` with `/api/orders/abc`: **400** (type mismatch). (ASP.NET Core route constraints give a 404 instead; Spring matches the route first and then fails to convert.)
- Malformed JSON body: **400** (`HttpMessageNotReadableException`). Wrong `Content-Type`: **415**.
- `@Valid` failing on a `@RequestBody`: **400**, from `MethodArgumentNotValidException`, which lists every field error.

## Validation

```java
public record CreateOrder(@NotBlank String sku, @Positive int quantity, @Email String contactEmail) {}
```

Jakarta Bean Validation annotations (`@NotNull`, `@NotBlank`, `@Size`, `@Min`, `@Positive`, `@Email`, `@Pattern`) check the request in isolation; business rules that need data (stock, uniqueness) belong in the service and usually map to 409 or 422.

With the record above, a body of `{"sku": "", "quantity": 0}` breaks two rules, a blank `sku` and a quantity that is not positive, and the 400 response lists both fields instead of stopping at the first. Most constraints treat `null` as valid, so `@Email` accepts a missing `contactEmail`; add `@NotNull` (or `@NotBlank`) when the field is required. Whether the SKU is in stock is never a Bean Validation question.

## Central error handling

```java
@RestControllerAdvice
public class ApiErrors {
    @ExceptionHandler(OrderNotFoundException.class)
    ProblemDetail notFound(OrderNotFoundException e) {
        return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, e.getMessage());
    }
    @ExceptionHandler(ConflictException.class)
    ProblemDetail conflict(ConflictException e) { return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.CONFLICT, e.getMessage()); }
    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    ProblemDetail unexpected(Exception e) {
        log.error("unhandled", e);
        return ProblemDetail.forStatus(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR);   // no internal details
    }
}
```

When several handlers could match, Spring picks the one for the **closest exception type** in the class hierarchy: `OrderNotFoundException extends NotFoundException extends RuntimeException` uses a `NotFoundException` handler before an `Exception` handler. `ProblemDetail` (Spring 6) is the RFC 9457 problem-details shape; in Spring Boot 3, `spring.mvc.problemdetails.enabled=true` makes the framework's own errors use it too.

Closeness is decided inside one handler set. Spring first tries the failing controller's own `@ExceptionHandler` methods, then the advice beans in `@Order` order, and the first advice with any matching handler wins: an `@Order(1)` advice that handles `Exception` answers an `OrderNotFoundException` before an `@Order(2)` advice that names that exact type. Drill 2 builds exactly this rule.

## Keep controllers thin

Bind, validate, call one service method, map the result. Transactions, business rules and persistence live below the controller, which keeps them testable without HTTP.

A thin handler is a few lines: `return service.create(body);` plus the `ResponseEntity` around it. If a controller method loops over repositories or computes prices, move that work into the service. A unit test of `OrderService` then needs no `MockMvc`, no JSON and no status codes, only `new OrderService(fakeRepo, fakeGateway)` and a method call.

## Entities

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "orders")
public class Order {
    @Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    @Column(nullable = false) private String status;
    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY) private Customer customer;
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<OrderLine> lines = new ArrayList<>();
    @Version private long version;                  // optimistic locking (topic 20)

    protected Order() {}                            // JPA needs a no-arg constructor
    public void addLine(OrderLine line) { lines.add(line); line.setOrder(this); }   // keep both sides in sync
}
```

JPA requires a no-argument constructor and non-final classes (for lazy proxies), and it mutates fields as it loads and tracks them: **records are not suitable as entities**. Use records for DTOs and projections.

Hibernate builds a lazy reference by subclassing the entity class at runtime, so the class must not be `final`. A record cannot be subclassed at all: with `record OrderDto(long id)`, a class declared as `extends OrderDto` does not compile ("cannot inherit from final OrderDto"). A record also has no no-argument constructor (`new OrderDto()` is a compile error), and its fields cannot be reassigned after construction.

## The persistence context and dirty checking

Inside a transaction, every loaded entity is **managed** by the persistence context (Hibernate's first-level cache):
- loading the same id twice returns the SAME instance
- changes to a managed entity are detected and written automatically at flush/commit (**dirty checking**): no `save()` call is needed for an entity you loaded in the same `@Transactional` method
- after the transaction ends, entities are **detached**: changes are no longer tracked

## Repositories

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {
    List<Order> findByStatusOrderByCreatedAtDesc(String status);
    Optional<Order> findByIdAndCustomerId(Long id, Long customerId);   // scope by owner: no IDOR
    boolean existsByCustomerEmail(String email);
    long countByStatus(String status);
    @Query("select o from Order o where o.total > :min")
    List<Order> findLarge(@Param("min") BigDecimal min);
}
```

Spring Data parses **derived query** method names (`find...By`, `count...By`, `exists...By`, `delete...By`, properties joined with `And` / `Or`, keywords like `GreaterThan`, `Containing`, `IgnoreCase`, `OrderBy...Desc`) into JPQL at startup; a misspelled property fails at startup. Drill 1 parses a subset of the same grammar: `findByStatusAndTotalGreaterThanOrderByCreatedAtDesc` gives the conditions status EQUALS and total GREATER_THAN, sorted by createdAt descending. A typo such as `findByStatsu` therefore stops the application context from starting, with an error that names the unknown property, long before any request calls it. Use `@Query` when a name would become unreadable.

`save(entity)` calls `persist` for a new entity and `merge` for an existing one. `merge` copies the given object's state onto a managed instance and returns THAT instance: keep using the returned object, not the argument.

Scope every customer-facing lookup by its owner. `findById(7L)` returns order 7 to any signed-in customer who guesses the id, an IDOR (insecure direct object reference). `findByIdAndCustomerId(7L, currentCustomerId)` returns `Optional.empty()` for someone else's order, and the controller answers 404, so the response does not even confirm that order 7 exists.

## Relationships

- The side with `mappedBy` is the inverse side; the side with the foreign key (`@ManyToOne`) **owns** the relationship. Only changes to the owning side are written.
- `cascade = ALL` propagates persist/remove from the parent to its children; `orphanRemoval = true` deletes a child removed from the collection.
- Bidirectional associations need helper methods that set both sides.

## equals and hashCode on entities

An auto-generated id is null until the entity is persisted, so equals/hashCode based on the id change at save time and break HashSets. Common choices: compare by a stable natural key (an order number, an email), or compare by id when non-null with a constant `hashCode`. Avoid Lombok `@Data` on entities (it generates equals/hashCode/toString over lazy associations).

Drill 2 shows the failure in plain Java: an `Order` added to a `HashSet` and then given id 42 is still found with a constant `hashCode`, but with `Objects.hash(id)` the set no longer finds it, because the set filed the entity under the hash it had while its id was null.

## A JUnit 5 test runs on a fresh instance

```java
class PriceCalculatorTest {
    private final FakeClock clock = new FakeClock(Instant.parse("2026-03-07T10:00:00Z"));
    private PriceCalculator calc;

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() { calc = new PriceCalculator(clock); }

    @Test
    void addsTheWeekendSurcharge() {
        assertEquals(new BigDecimal("12.00"), calc.price("SKU-1", 1));
    }

    @Test
    void rejectsANegativeQuantity() {
        var e = assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class, () -> calc.price("SKU-1", -1));
        assertEquals("quantity must be positive", e.getMessage());
    }
}
```

By default JUnit Jupiter creates a NEW instance of the test class for every test method, so instance fields start fresh each time. `@BeforeEach` runs before every test, `@BeforeAll` once per class (a static method by default). The order of test methods is not something to rely on: a test that needs another test to run first is broken. `assertThrows` returns the exception so you can check its message, `assertAll` reports every failing assertion instead of the first, and `@ParameterizedTest` with `@CsvSource` runs one test over many rows.

## Test behaviour through the public API

A good test says what the code promises: given these inputs, this result, this state, this message sent. It calls the public methods and checks outcomes. A test that asserts private details or the order of internal calls breaks on every refactor and still misses real bugs.

## Mockito: stubs, defaults and verification

```java
PaymentGateway gateway = mock(PaymentGateway.class);
when(gateway.charge("order-7", "card-1", 1200)).thenReturn(new Charge("ch_1", "card-1", 1200, APPROVED));

checkout.pay("7", "card-1", 1200);

verify(mailer, times(1)).send(eq("ana@x.io"), contains("receipt"));
```

A stub answers only a call whose arguments are `equals` to the stubbed ones; any other call gets the default answer: `null` for most objects, `0` or `false` for primitives and their wrappers, and an EMPTY collection, `Optional.empty()` or empty stream for those types. Once one argument uses a matcher (`any()`, `eq(...)`), every argument must. `thenThrow` scripts a failure, and an `ArgumentCaptor` catches what was passed so you can assert on it. With `MockitoExtension`'s strict stubs, a stub the test never uses fails the test, which keeps setups honest.

## Fake the boundary; do not mock what you do not own

Put a seam at the edge of your system, an interface YOU define (`PaymentGateway`, `Mailer`, a `Clock`), and mock or fake that. Do not mock types you do not own, such as a JDBC `ResultSet`, an `HttpClient` or JPA's `EntityManager`: the mock encodes your guess of how they behave, and the tests stay green when the guess is wrong. Test those against the real thing (an in-memory or containerised database behind your repository).

A hand-written fake of your own boundary is often better than a mock: it is reusable, readable, and it can keep the real rules. A payment fake that honours idempotency keys and can "lose" a response after charging lets a test prove that the retry does not charge twice.

## Spies and over-mocking

`spy(real)` calls the real methods unless stubbed. `when(spy.loadAll()).thenReturn(...)` calls the REAL `loadAll` once while setting up the stub; use `doReturn(...).when(spy).loadAll()` instead. If a test needs ten `when` lines and then verifies every call, it is a copy of the implementation: it breaks on every refactor and proves little. Prefer one fake and assertions on the outcome.

## Constraints live on the DTO

```java
public record CreateOrder(
    @NotNull @Valid Address shipTo,
    @NotEmpty @Size(max = 50) List<@Valid OrderLine> lines,
    @Size(max = 500) String note,
    @NotBlank @Email String contactEmail) {}

@PostMapping("/orders")
ResponseEntity<OrderDto> create(@Valid @RequestBody CreateOrder body) { ... }
```

Jakarta Bean Validation reads annotations on fields, getters or record components. In a Spring MVC controller, `@Valid` on a `@RequestBody` parameter runs the validator after the JSON is bound; any violation throws `MethodArgumentNotValidException` before your method runs, and Spring answers 400. The validator reports EVERY violation it finds, not just the first one, so one round trip tells the client everything to fix.

## NotNull, NotEmpty and NotBlank are three different rules

`@NotNull` only refuses `null`. `@NotEmpty` also refuses an empty string or collection. `@NotBlank` is for strings and refuses `null`, `""` and whitespace such as `"   "`. Every other built-in constraint treats `null` as VALID: `@Size(min = 2)`, `@Email`, `@Pattern` and `@Min` pass a missing value. This is deliberate (each constraint checks one thing), so a required field needs `@NotNull` or `@NotBlank` next to its format rules. On a primitive `int`, `@NotNull` does nothing: a missing JSON value is already `0` (topic 23), and only `@Min(1)` notices.

## Nothing cascades unless you say @Valid

A constraint on `Address.city` is not checked when you validate an `Order` that holds an `Address`, unless the `shipTo` component is marked `@Valid`. The same goes for collections: `List<@Valid OrderLine>` (or `@Valid` on the list) validates each element. Violations then carry a property path such as `shipTo.city` or `lines[2].quantity`, which is what the client should see next to each field.

## Method validation goes through a proxy

Outside controllers, `@Validated` on a Spring bean turns on method validation: constraint annotations on its method parameters and return values are checked by a proxy (`MethodValidationPostProcessor`) and a violation throws `ConstraintViolationException`. Like `@Transactional` and `@PreAuthorize` (topics 20 and 22), a call on `this` inside the bean skips the proxy and skips the checks. Validate at the edge where data enters, and treat service-level checks as a second line, not the only one.

## Custom and cross-field constraints

A rule the built-ins cannot express gets its own annotation and a `ConstraintValidator<A, T>`. Follow the convention and return `true` for `null`, leaving "required" to `@NotNull`. A rule over two fields, such as "end date after start date", is a class-level constraint whose validator sees the whole object; attach its violation to the field that should be highlighted. Keep business rules that need the database (is this coupon still valid?) in the service: a validator should be a fast, pure check.

## Groups: one DTO, two rule sets

`@NotNull(groups = OnUpdate.class) Long id` applies only when that group is validated, for example with `@Validated(OnUpdate.class)`. Constraints without a group belong to `Default`, and validating `OnUpdate` alone does NOT check them unless `OnUpdate extends Default`. Groups are easy to get subtly wrong; two small DTOs, one for create and one for update, are often clearer.

## A 400 the client can act on

Map violations to one response: status 400, a stable title, and per field the list of messages (Spring's `ProblemDetail` with an `errors` extension works well). Echoing the rejected value helps for a quantity, but never for a password, a token or a card number: those values end up in logs and screenshots.

## Parameterised messages, not string building

```java
private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(OrderService.class);

log.debug("pricing order {} with {} lines", order.id(), order.lines().size());
log.warn("payment {} declined for order {}", paymentId, orderId);
log.error("could not capture payment {}", paymentId, e);   // e goes last: printed with its stack trace
```

SLF4J fills each `{}` with the next argument's `toString()`, and only when the level is enabled. `log.debug("total " + expensive())` builds the string and calls `expensive()` even when debug is off; the parameterised form does neither (an argument that is itself costly to compute still needs `if (log.isDebugEnabled())` or a supplier in SLF4J 2's fluent API). A `{}` with no argument left is printed as it is, extra arguments are ignored, and an escaped placeholder, written `"\\{}"` in Java source, prints a literal `{}`.

## The exception goes last, and only once

Pass the exception as the last argument, after the values for every `{}`: SLF4J treats a trailing `Throwable` as the exception to print with its stack trace. Writing `"failed: " + e` keeps only the message and loses the stack. Log an exception where you handle it; logging it and then rethrowing it prints the same failure once per layer, and the real cause is hard to find in the duplicates.

## Levels say who must act

ERROR: someone must act now, a request or job failed. WARN: something degraded but was handled (a fallback, a retry that later worked). INFO: business events worth counting (order placed, payment captured). DEBUG: detail for developers, off in production by default. A level is a promise to whoever is on call; a log full of ERRORs nobody acts on hides the one that matters.

## MDC: context every line can print

The Mapped Diagnostic Context is a per-thread map of key/value pairs. Put the request id in it once, at the edge, and a pattern such as `%X{requestId}` (or a JSON encoder) prints it on every log line that thread writes, without passing the id to every method. Accept an incoming `X-Request-Id` only if it matches a strict format, otherwise generate one: a raw header copied into logs lets a caller inject fake log lines. Return the id in the response so a user's bug report can name it.

## Set it at the edge, clear it in finally

```java
MDC.put("requestId", id);
try {
    chain.doFilter(request, response);
} finally {
    MDC.remove("requestId");
}
```

Server threads are pooled. A context that is not removed in `finally` stays on the thread and labels the NEXT request's lines with the wrong id, the same leak as an uncleared security context (topic 22). `MDC.putCloseable` in a try-with-resources does the same in one line.

## MDC does not follow work to another thread

Work handed to an executor, a `CompletableFuture` or a scheduled job runs on a thread with its own, usually empty, context. Capture `MDC.getCopyOfContextMap()` where the task is created, install it with `MDC.setContextMap` around the task, and put the worker's previous context back afterwards. Spring's `TaskDecorator` is the hook for this in a `ThreadPoolTaskExecutor`.

## Never log secrets

Passwords, tokens, full card numbers and session cookies do not belong in logs, at any level: logs are copied to more places and kept longer than databases. Log ids, not payloads; mask what must appear (`****4242`); and make `toString()` of a credentials type safe, because `log.info("login {}", credentials)` calls it.
